Subject: [PATCH] crypto: avoid error initialization on pthread lock allocation failure

CRYPTO_THREAD_lock_new deliberately does not set errors because it is used
while OpenSSL base and error-state once initializers are active. The allocation
helpers now report failure automatically, so OPENSSL_zalloc can re-enter the
same initializer and deadlock before lock_new returns NULL.

Use the existing NULL-file/zero-line suppression contract for these low-level
lock allocations, as already used when allocating ERR_STATE. Keep the NULL
failure return and cleanup; callers remain responsible for reporting errors
once error initialization is available.

--- a/crypto/threads_pthread.c
+++ b/crypto/threads_pthread.c
@@ -907,7 +907,7 @@
 
     ossl_init_rwlock_contention_data();
 
-    if ((lock = OPENSSL_zalloc(sizeof(pthread_rwlock_t))) == NULL)
+    if ((lock = CRYPTO_zalloc(sizeof(pthread_rwlock_t), NULL, 0)) == NULL)
         /* Don't set error, to avoid recursion blowup. */
         return NULL;
 
@@ -919,7 +919,7 @@
     pthread_mutexattr_t attr;
     CRYPTO_RWLOCK *lock;
 
-    if ((lock = OPENSSL_zalloc(sizeof(pthread_mutex_t))) == NULL)
+    if ((lock = CRYPTO_zalloc(sizeof(pthread_mutex_t), NULL, 0)) == NULL)
         /* Don't set error, to avoid recursion blowup. */
         return NULL;
 
